# L5R 5E - FIELDS OF VICTORY: THE LION CLAN (LORE)

**Version:** 0.4
**Source:** Legend of the Five Rings Roleplaying Game, 5th Edition - Fields of Victory (Chapter 1)
**Publisher:** Fantasy Flight Games

---

## THE LION CLAN

The Lion Clan stands at the geographic and spiritual heart of the Emerald Empire. Their provinces occupy central Rokugan, and their purpose has always been singular: the art and practice of war. Since its founding, the Lion Clan has served as the Emperor's foremost military instrument, but its samurai are more than soldiers. They are the custodians of Rokugan's martial heritage, the living embodiment of the warrior tradition, and a constant reminder that every samurai is, before all else, a warrior.

The Kami Akodo forged the Lion into the blade of the Emperor, poised to strike or defend at his sovereign's command. The Lion see themselves as the Empire's warrior conscience, holding all samurai to the standards of Bushido. They are scholars of warfare who have produced many of the finest tacticians the Empire has ever known, and they are the staunchest allies of the Hantei line, ever ready at the Emperor's side, just as Akodo stood beside Hantei at the dawn of the Empire.

The Emperor's first commands to the Lion are carved into the foundation of every Lion dojo: to be his sword and armor, his living memory, and his lantern in the night. The Lion interpret these ancient orders as their threefold ancestral duty -- to serve as the military arm of the Emperor, to preserve and champion Rokugan's warrior traditions, and to remind the Emperor's servants that they are samurai above all.

Among the Lion's most profound contributions to Rokugani culture is the codification of Bushido, the way of the warrior. Before Akodo, notions of warriors' honor existed among many groups but varied widely and were rarely written down. Akodo defined Bushido formally in his seminal text Leadership, outlining a code of seven virtues shared by all honorable warriors. Though the Lion often behave as though they invented the code, Akodo himself acknowledged that Bushido is an innate truth known by instinct to all honorable warriors, and that ancient heroes walked its path long before it had a name.

The Lion practice ancestor worship with a fervor that surpasses all other clans. Their traditions stretch further back than most, and they remember not merely the greatest heroes but every person born into the family. Each Lion family maintains ancestral records of all its members and presents offerings to them all. Lion household shrines are more elaborate and crowded than those of any other clan, and in the wealthiest homes, the shrine occupies a separate building with halls dedicated to especially heroic figures. The Lion hold more ancestral festivals than any other clan and believe that a Lion warrior never fights alone but stands with generations of ancestors at their side.

The Lion also worship the Fortunes, especially Bishamon (Fortune of Strength) and Hachiman (Fortune of Warfare), but they consider Fortune worship an entirely separate religious practice. Fortune worship is transactional, while ancestor veneration is a sacred obligation.

---

## FAMILIES OF THE LION

The four great families of the Lion date from the dawn of the Empire. The Akodo guide the clan as its moral compass and traditional leaders, tracing their ancestry directly to the Kami Akodo himself. Steady at their side are the warlike Matsu, who supply the bulk of the Lion's military forces as the clan's largest samurai family. Together, the Akodo and Matsu lead the clan, united by mutual respect but differing in vision and philosophy. The adventurous and studious Ikoma are the Lion's scholars and diplomatic face, keeping the clan's histories, advising its elders, and representing the Lion at court. Finally, the Kitsu family of shugenja is the clan's spiritual anchor, its connection to the ancestors and the will of the Heavens.

The hierarchy of Lion families is as clearly defined as an army's military ranks. However, all four families have led the Lion at some point in history. Most often an Akodo or Matsu has served as Lion Clan Champion, less commonly an Ikoma, and a handful of times, a Kitsu. When no clear heir exists or the prior champion has not named a successor, the clan's leader is ultimately whoever commands the strongest claim, and what that means has shifted from era to era.

---

### The Akodo Family

To establish his clan, the Kami Akodo sought out the greatest fighters and tactical minds, challenging them in whatever they claimed as their mastery -- duels, tests of strength, games of wit and strategy. Few proved his equal, and his clan grew slowly. While his siblings boasted the allegiance of entire families and their clans began to swell, Akodo had only a small fellowship of warriors, tightly knit but few in number. He did not mind, knowing his patience would pay off. One winter, when he brought only a handful to court before the Emperor, Hantei remarked that Akodo was a lion without a pride. Akodo simply replied that a lion does not stand with sheep.

**Reputation and Values:** Simply following Bushido is insufficient for the Akodo; it must be fully embraced, all tenets given equal weight. If forced to elevate one virtue above the rest, the Akodo choose Honor. They believe a true samurai acts honorably by nature -- dishonorable action never occurs to them, right action is obvious, and any disgrace is unthinkable. Such a warrior, the Akodo insist, cannot be defeated.

This philosophy earns praise from other clans but also explains the Akodo's reputation as unyielding and overly principled. They are notoriously difficult negotiators, far less pragmatic than their Ikoma cousins. The Akodo famously trust only outsiders they have personally faced in contest, duel, or skirmish, believing a person's true nature is revealed in battle when everything is stripped away and essential values are laid bare.

The Akodo remember all slights and let nothing slide. An Akodo might remind another samurai that their ancestor insulted the Lion centuries ago and then challenge that samurai on the spot. They will reject an alliance, however strategically sound, until the potential ally has apologized for some ancestor's role in leaving a blemish on Akodo history, no matter how minor the offense.

**Culture and Traditions:** Akodo culture emphasizes the group over the individual, teaching children from a young age to prioritize the greater needs of the family. An Akodo's path is not chosen by themselves but by parents, advisors, teachers, and lord -- and, many insist, by the ancestors who the Akodo believe are a constant presence, always watching, guiding, and judging.

At the heart of Akodo culture is warrior fellowship, embodied in the concept of the doshi, or dojo partner. While most dojo pair students together as doshi, among the Akodo this is a sacred bond. Doshi are not merely classmates and sparring partners but blood siblings in spirit, bound by a lifelong commitment whose ties are as strong as a karmic link. Doshi are expected to remain loyal to one another no matter the circumstance and to support each other in their weakest moments, even long into adulthood.

The most valued path in Akodo culture is that of the general. The very word Akodo invokes generations of accomplished war generals and brilliant tacticians. The most prestigious school is the Akodo War College, where the traditions and techniques of the Akodo Commander School are taught. Acceptance assures a long and celebrated career, as the War College is the most reputable institute of leadership and tactics in Rokugan, claiming the most successful army generals in history. Its halls are decorated with war banners taken from defeated generals of other clans, and the school maintains an open challenge for anyone to come reclaim them.

The Lion maintain an exceptional internal road network. Rather than dividing road maintenance by ancestral territory, the entire clan collaborates to keep its internal roads in prime condition, laying them out for rapid troop deployment. These roads are among the best engineered in the Empire. By contrast, the roads along the Lion's borders are intentionally left in disrepair to frustrate would-be invaders.

The Lion's relationship with the Tao of Shinsei is unique among the Great Clans. Akodo found Shinsei's philosophy so antithetical to the samurai way that he denounced it before the Emperor and the Little Teacher himself, forbidding all members of his clan from so much as glancing at its pages. However, Hantei intervened and ordered that a copy of the Tao be given a place of honor within every Lion dojo. Akodo could not disobey. To this day, every Lion dojo has a copy of Shinsei's Tao displayed beside its copy of Leadership -- and to this day, every Lion dojo's copy of the Tao remains unopened.

**Lands:** The Lion's lands were once the domain Akodo conquered for himself. Continually ready to defend the capital, Akodo built his keep a mere day's ride from Otosan Uchi. The Akodo provinces are dominated by rolling grassy plains, with streams originating from the Empire's two largest rivers working their way across the land like thin veins, nourishing an abundance of grains, shrubs, and flowering plants.

Shiro Akodo, or Loyalty Castle, is the ancestral seat of Akodo power and the greatest of the family's fortresses. Positioned a day's ride from Otosan Uchi across mostly flat ground, it allows the Lion Clan Champion to respond swiftly to any threat to the capital, which is always visible on the horizon. The castle maintains the clan's largest staging grounds and retinue of smithies, constantly cycling and drilling troops and keeping its forges burning day and night. As a keep built for military campaigns, Loyalty Castle was never designed for diplomacy. Not even the Akodo daimyo's personal estate is on the grounds, and the castle itself is austere, its labyrinthine layout designed to confuse invaders, its steps famously uneven to trip rushing attackers. However, once a year, Loyalty Castle hosts the grandest Go tournament in the Empire, crowning Rokugan's Go champion after a week-long competition.

The Heart of Vigilance Shrine lies within an ancient forest that remained untouched for ages due to rumors that its pines and bamboo thickets were blessed by the Fortunes. When centuries of deforestation elsewhere forced the Akodo to harvest the forest's lumber, bushi discovered a forgotten shrine within, dedicated to forty-two Lesser Fortunes. The shrine appeared to predate the Imperial proclamations recognizing those very Fortunes, and despite its obvious age, its architecture was modern Rokugani in style. No records could identify its builders or purpose. Within weeks of the initial harvest, every directing bushi died suddenly. To this day, foresters avoid the trees closest to the shrine and draw wood from the Heart of Vigilance Forest only in times of dire need, for those who take the sacred wood invariably perish soon after.

Ninkatoshi, or Authorized City, has rapidly grown over the past century from a simple farming village at the mouth of the Golden Valley into a full-fledged city. Historically, this is where travelers awaited permission to continue into Akodo provinces. The growing non-resident presence has given rise to marketplaces, teahouses, and entertainment establishments catering to travelers of all kinds.

---

### The Matsu Family

While many warriors strove to meet Akodo's challenges, one denied him. The warrior Matsu had earned a fierce reputation as a bandit lord who targeted those she deemed unjust and corrupt. One day, recognizing the harm she had caused to the meekest commoners, she cast aside banditry and distributed her ill-gotten wealth to suffering villages, settling down to open a martial school. When her former followers came seeking vengeance for her betrayal, each of them ended up buried in the yard of her school. Word of this reformed bandit reached Akodo's ears, and he was intrigued.

Matsu ignored Akodo's many requests for an audience, viewing him as just another brute rising at the cost of others. Eventually he came to her village with his forces, offering her a place by his side as his wife. She countered with a challenge to duel. When they finished, Akodo lay on his back with Matsu's blade at his throat. That same day, Matsu pledged herself to Akodo's cause, on the condition that they be equals in the clan. Her descendants believe the duel revealed Akodo's honor and ambition, and she judged him worthy of her respect and loyalty, believing that their combined skills would take them further than either could rise alone.

**Reputation and Values:** The Matsu name is synonymous throughout the Empire with both the best and worst of the Lion Clan, embodying its greatest strengths and its greatest weaknesses. This reputation stems from the Matsu's aversion to hesitation. Boldness and impetuous action are considered virtuous in Matsu culture. The ideal state of the warrior is one in which there is no space between thought and deed, and the warrior becomes a vessel for pure martial instinct. If one does not commit fully to action, consequences and all, one cannot call oneself a samurai.

The Matsu believe their honor is their greatest weapon. Bushido is not merely a code but an innate truth of the universe, the distilled mindset of the warrior. The Matsu do not view its virtues as constraints but as sources of strength, which accounts for their inspirational leadership style -- a Matsu general always plunges into the thickest fighting, galvanizing everyone around them.

For all their renown in honor and battle prowess, the Matsu are most famous for their powerful emotions. Other clans consider a short and mighty temper their defining trait. The Matsu battle rage, an entranced state in which a warrior becomes one with martial instinct and immune to hesitation, fear, and pain, is the hallmark of their school. A common saying holds that the Akodo do not forget, but the Matsu do not forgive.

**Culture and Traditions:** Matsu culture derives from the life of their legendary founder, one of the Seven Thunders. The Matsu seek to emulate her in all things, from the courts to the battlefield. Whether through art, poetry, games, or martial training, everything the Matsu do is fully committed and viewed through the lens of the warrior mind. Matsu poetry is spontaneous and improvised, composed from cutting words and delivered with unflinching emotion. Matsu theater favors the Kabuki style, filled with action, passion, and special effects, with directors treating their troupes like military units drilling relentlessly for a flawless performance. Other samurai say that no matter what a Matsu attempts, they are sharpening their claws.

From the age of six, Matsu children are trained in lethal combat. Those who do not take to martial arts are swiftly placed elsewhere -- fostered to other families or sent to monastic life. Even Matsu who become artists and courtiers conduct themselves as warriors, destined for a very different battlefield.

The Matsu gempuku is considered draconian even by Crane or Scorpion standards. Students must pass many tests to reach adulthood, including reciting memorized passages from Leadership, proving martial prowess with a variety of weapons, quartering a tossed pomegranate before it touches the ground, and enduring numerous strikes from a bamboo practice sword against their bare back. Those who pass are marked with the Matsu crest as a sign of eternal devotion. Those who fail are permitted to commit seppuku to spare themselves and their parents the shame. Even in this harsh family, however, familial love endures. A Matsu parent who fears their child will not pass the gempuku often arranges to have the child adopted by another family or enter a monastic order, preserving the tradition without condemning the child.

The kincho tradition demonstrates Matsu sincerity. Since a Matsu's word and deed are as one, promises are considered unnecessary, and to ask for one is an insult. However, when Matsu samurai wish to seal an oath, they strike their swords together in kincho. It is believed that if one party is lying, their sword will break and they will be cut or killed by the other.

Matsu lords and vassal families constantly seek to expand their territory. Limited conflicts between Matsu houses are common and even encouraged, as the family believes internal warring keeps them strong. Matsu leaders cannot show any sign of weakness, lest neighbors sense an opening to seize their lands.

The Matsu Beastmasters are among the most unusual martial traditions in the Empire. These warriors train lions and other animals to fight alongside Lion soldiers in battle. A Beastmaster is always accompanied by at least one trained animal, raised alongside them since adolescence, with which they share a bond of mutual respect. Caring for the lions of the Matsu is considered a sacred task, for legends claim these lions carry the spirits of ancient kitsu, accounting for their unusually high intelligence.

The Deathseeker tradition originates in disgrace and glorious redemption. During a border dispute between the Lion and Scorpion, a Matsu lord betrayed his liege and sided with the Scorpion. His retainers rebelled, took his head, and served in the vanguard to atone for their lord's treachery. The sole survivor, Kirifu, swore to serve in the army's most dangerous positions to spare others. Her oath became a path of atonement through selfless action. Deathseekers return their katana, wakizashi, and family relics, surrender rank and holdings, and swear to a warrior pilgrimage, disregarding their own safety and confronting necessary dangers so others need not. They can be identified by their black-and-white painted faces, braided cords, and relative lack of armor. While many admire the tradition, it remains controversial -- some view severing oneself from lord and family as selfish rather than selfless, and no one is ever pleased to hear a loved one has joined the Deathseeker ranks.

**Lands:** Matsu territories unfurl across Lion provinces like a waving banner. Oxen and lion prides roam the flatlands, while herds of deer wander dense forests. The lands grow hillier to the south and west, culminating in the Spine of the World Mountains, whose foothills house rich copper mines, one of the clan's most important exports. To the southwest lies Beiden Pass, one of the few crossroads through the mountain range and part of the Empire's most important trade route. Along the border with the Crane, Matsu lands nearly reach the open gates of Kyuden Kakita, and the territory has changed hands countless times over the centuries.

The Hall of Ancestors, built into the western hills above Shiro Matsu, is the most sacred site of the Lion Clan. Jointly maintained by the Matsu and Kitsu families, it contains the remains of the clan's greatest heroes. Its many tombs, shrines, and memorials compose the largest funerary complex in the Empire, and to be enshrined there is one of the Lion's highest honors. The complex also houses one of the richest collections of genealogical information available to the clan. Traditionally, every Lion samurai visits this place at least once in their lifetime.

Kenson no Gakka, or Humility's Lesson, is the largest Lion keep along the mountainous Scorpion border. Originally belonging to the Ikoma, the castle was infiltrated and seized by the Scorpion Clan in the sixth century. The Scorpion renamed it Castle of Honor, intending to use it as leverage in a campaign against Sacred Watch Palace. However, the Matsu stormed the keep, killed every Scorpion within, then pressed into Scorpion territory through the southern Beiden Pass, eventually coming within sight of Kyuden Bayushi before Imperial intervention halted their advance. Since then, the Matsu have held the castle and renamed it Humility's Lesson. They celebrate an annual festival on the anniversary of its recapture, with imported fireworks and boisterous song deliberately visible and audible from Scorpion lands.

Shiro Matsu, or Last Breath Castle, overlooks Beiden Pass from a rising sea of golden grass near the Lake of Sorrows. It is the home of the Matsu family daimyo and the center of the family's military power. It was built at the historic site where Lady Matsu first met the Kami Akodo. The nearby settlement, once the tiny village Matsu called home, has grown into the largest and most industrious town in Lion lands. Pilgrims from across the provinces arrive on their way to the Hall of Ancestors, which Last Breath Castle protects. Were the Akodo not the traditional leaders of the clan, Last Breath Castle would be the Lion capital. Unlike Loyalty Castle, Shiro Matsu hosts diplomats year-round, though few concessions are made to a guest's comfort, and most courtiers consider an assignment there a severe punishment.

The Valley of Storms lies to the north of Last Breath Castle and forms the breadbasket of the Matsu family. To the south, Beiden Pass lies exposed to Matsu scouts and way stations; any travelers entering without Matsu permission are immediately discovered, though those seeking passage into Crane lands to the south are generally allowed through.

---

### The Ikoma Family

Akodo found many of his greatest warriors in dojo and at crossroads duels, but his closest ally, the elder Ikoma, he found drunk in a sake house. The only patron who refused to bow to the divine Kami, Ikoma met each of Akodo's threats with one in turn and topped each of Akodo's boasted feats with several of his own. When Akodo expressed disbelief at what he saw as a doddering old man's claims, Ikoma regaled the Kami with battle tales of his youth well into the night. By morning, Akodo had warmed to the old man and offered him a place at his side. The founder of the Ikoma family is remembered as a braggart and a wastrel, a brawler and a boaster, and an old fossil quick to speak his mind -- but also as a charismatic storyteller, a wise advisor, a mischievous ally, and one of the few mortals Akodo called friend.

**Reputation and Values:** Outside the Lion Clan, the Ikoma have a reputation as the Lion's resident troublemakers, an impressive feat given that the clan also contains the impetuous Matsu. This reputation owes partly to the infamy of their founder, who notoriously preferred brothels and sake houses to dojo and tea gardens and fought with his bare hands against armed opponents. Despite all this, the Ikoma are generally regarded as the most approachable Lion family.

The Ikoma teach that pride is a virtue. The past should be venerated, one's ancestors celebrated, and one's deeds eagerly shared. Bragging is actively encouraged, for when a person is recognized for their accomplishments, so too are their ancestors honored. Since a samurai's name is inherited, each samurai's personal goal should be to return it more glorious than before.

**Culture and Traditions:** The Ikoma way is that of the adventurer-scholar, embracing knowledge and martial prowess in equal measure. Ikoma culture molds its members to serve as military strategists, scholars, experts in law, and representatives of the clan. They are charged with keeping the official clan histories, a daunting duty that includes recording samurai genealogies, interpretations of law, military innovations, victories, and -- perhaps most importantly -- defeats. Neutral Ikoma heralds are regularly deployed to both sides of momentous battles, honor-bound and sworn against interference, in order to record events precisely as they unfold. The Ikoma have even been known to accept written accounts of battles as marriage gifts or bargaining chips in negotiations.

The Ikoma Libraries, housed at Kyuden Ikoma within an ever-expanding shrine to Tenjin (the Lesser Fortune of Stories and Secrets), are the largest in the Empire and the most expensive, time-consuming endeavor of the family. The documents within are tended with the same reverence as religious texts. Officially, the Libraries house the records used by the Imperial families, who have open access at all times. If it is not in the Ikoma Libraries, the Ikoma insist it never happened. That the records within always seem to portray the Imperial line -- and, often, the Lion Clan -- in the most flattering light is beside the point. Few Rokugani dare contradict the handpicked accounts within, lest they inadvertently offend those who pen the histories embraced by the Emperor himself.

The Ikoma are most famous for their bardic tradition. Ikoma bards, known as omoidasu, are part historian, part folklorist, part adventurer, and part performer. Emotion is what sets them apart from other storytellers. They are among the few in Rokugan permitted to display open emotion in court without loss of clout, so accepted is their social role. They serve as voices of the voiceless, accusers on behalf of the wronged, and boasters on behalf of the accomplished. Often an Ikoma bard argues a Lion samurai's case in court, passionately recounting slights endured, while the injured party remains serene beside them. At funerals, the bard recalls the deeds of the departed and openly mourns, expressing the grief others cannot, allowing the loved ones of the deceased to maintain face even amid overwhelming tragedy.

These bards wander the lands seeking new tales, following warrior companies and joining honor guards, serving as mediators and advisors while collecting stories. By reminding Rokugan's samurai of their best, the omoidasu hope to show them how great they can be.

The Ikoma also maintain a covert intelligence network known as the Lion's Shadow. While the Akodo and Matsu hold themselves to strict standards, the Ikoma understand the ugly realities of warfare: prolonged conflict is bad for the Empire, and needless loss of life shames the Emperor. More pragmatically, they understand the power of information to determine a battle's outcome. The Lion's Shadow is a small network of spies and infiltrators who gather intelligence and, sometimes, end battles before they begin. Select students are trained in subterfuge and stealth to serve as spymasters and saboteurs. This network is not widely known within the Lion, but it is obvious to any general that military leaders have access to enemy troop numbers, deployments, and battle plans. Akodo himself endorsed espionage in warfare, rewarding his spies more handsomely than his highest-ranking advisors to ensure their loyalty. The Ikoma are quick to point out the distinction between shinobi, who are outlawed by Imperial edict, and spies, whom the law never mentions.

**Lands:** The Venerable Plains of the Ikoma occupy the northeast corner of Lion territory, a dusty region of rolling hills, wild grasses, bamboo groves, and signal towers along minor rivers. The terrain resembles that of the Lion's Unicorn neighbors, suitable for the clan's meager pony herds. Ikoma lands are relatively sparse throughout, more heavily populated at their borders, especially to the south where they keep a wary eye on the Scorpion.

Kyuden Ikoma, or Sacred Watch Palace, is the ancestral seat of the Ikoma family, standing just beneath the face of the Mountain of Seven Thunders. The castle was originally built as the primary signal tower for the Lion's western border. While suitable for hosting diplomatic meetings and the Emperor's family, it remains defensible and retains its original military purpose. Within the keep are the vast Ikoma Libraries, the pride of the family and the envy of historical scholars throughout the Empire.

The City of Honor's Sacrifice is among the oldest cities in Ikoma territory, dominating the northern Firefly Plains along the Drowned Merchant River, making it rich with trade from Unicorn and Dragon travelers. It also boasts the greatest geisha house in Lion lands, the House of Morning Dew. The city's name derives from the legend of Ikoma Arimi, a Lion samurai whose sterling reputation was ruined when her lone indiscretion was exposed by her enemies. When she requested ritual suicide to cleanse her family of shame, her spiteful lord gave her a wooden practice sword to perform the ceremony, intending to cast her under further disgrace. Regardless, she impaled herself upon the blunt weapon, erasing all doubt as to her honor and shaming all who had disbelieved her. A statue to her memory still watches over the Firefly Plains.

---

### The Kitsu Family

When Akodo first set out to tame the heartlands of Rokugan, he encountered a feline warrior people known as the kitsu -- powerful supernatural beings with aspects of both humans and lions who had existed in the Empire's heartlands since time immemorial. According to the Kitsu family, Akodo's war against the kitsu people began when he and his followers mistook the lionlike beings for monsters. As the final battle loomed and the extermination of the kitsu seemed inevitable, a single kitsu made their way to Akodo's war tent. Instead of killing the enemy general, the kitsu offered surrender. This shocking act caused Akodo to realize the kitsu were intelligent and possessed the same sense of honor as he. According to legend, Akodo apologized to the last of the kitsu people and offered them a place within his clan. Those who accepted took human form, founding the Kitsu family.

**Reputation and Values:** Members of the Kitsu family are rarely encountered beyond Lion borders. Other Rokugani regard them as unusually fierce for shugenja, blunt in manner, and strangely comfortable with death. Among the principles of Bushido, the Kitsu most highly venerate Honor, Compassion, and Righteousness.

The Kitsu bow to Heaven first. Their duties to ancestors come before their duties to the Lion Clan, for they know it is the Fortunes and ancestors who judge them first and finally. Theirs is a calling greater than the concerns of individual mortals, which sometimes sets them at odds with the Akodo and Matsu -- but only the truly foolish would stand in a sodan-senzo's way.

Few things offend a Kitsu like speaking ill of the dead. They believe a person's karma is left behind when they pass on, and while Emma-O judges mortals by their deeds in life, spirits are ultimately redeemed by the Realm of Punishment. The Kitsu honor even angry spirits and seek to settle their grudges.

The Kitsu make no attempt to hide the tragic and terrible origins of their family, believing that embellishing or obscuring history shows disrespect to those who lived through it. They care little for recorded history as kept by others. Instead, they commune with the ancestors to learn what actually happened, and they are far more likely to embrace controversial ideas than most Rokugani. Their written records tend to be unadorned, simple, and uncomfortably frank.

**Culture and Traditions:** Of all Lion family traditions, the ways of the Kitsu are the most unusual, derived in part from the customs of their feline ancestors. The Kitsu family believes it is their duty to keep the ways of the original kitsu people alive, a promise Akodo made in atonement for the misunderstanding that destroyed them.

Kitsu family members with close ancestral connections to the kitsu bloodline inherit the physical traits of their feline forebears. Golden eyes are such a trait, as are elongated canines, bushy eyebrows, and thick red manes of hair. Not all Kitsu inherit these features, but within the family they are regarded not only as marks of beauty but as evidence of strong ties to the kitsu bloodline.

The most important sign of kitsu heritage is an innate connection to spirits and ancestors. The original kitsu's abilities primarily involved peering into the Spirit Realms, giving physical form to ghosts, channeling ancestors, and similar feats. The greatest among them could physically cross into the Spirit Realms to confer with the dead. Those Kitsu whose connection to the bloodline is strong inherit these abilities. A Kitsu Medium can call upon the dead, grant them spectral form in the Realm of Mortals, and even allow the dead to possess the medium's body temporarily. Through special ceremonies, Kitsu Realm Wanderers can enter Yomi (the Realm of Blessed Ancestors), Meido (the Realm of Waiting), and other Spirit Realms. These traits are why the Kitsu are known as the voices of the dead, allowing ancestors to speak directly to their descendants -- a gift rarely held by other shugenja.

The Kitsu are notoriously insular, isolating themselves even from other Lion families. They forbid outsiders to study Kitsu ways and consider revealing their secret traditions to be akin to betrayal. This applies even to those who marry into the family, although individuals eventually judged worthy may be allowed a glimpse behind the curtain. The Kitsu never reveal the secrets of the sodan-senzo to anyone who cannot prove they have inherited the kitsu bloodline abilities. No Lion would disgrace the Kitsu by requesting a place in their secret ceremonies, while to be offered a seat is a gesture of profound trust and a great honor.

Birth into the Kitsu is no guarantee that one will possess ancestral abilities. Kitsu matchmakers are primarily concerned with preserving the ancestral connection, but many children seem to lack the desired capacity, and true sodan-senzo grow rarer with each generation. Family members who lack this potential usually become bureaucrats, courtiers, shrine keepers, or scholars through training in other Lion schools. The Kitsu family also maintains the Kitsu Medic School to place many of its people as battlefield medics in Lion Clan units.

**Lands:** Kitsu family territory lies at the heart of Lion lands, protected by other Lion territory on all sides but the north. These are the most fertile of the Lion's provinces, boasting the most numerous and prosperous farms and orchards, owing in part to the blessings invoked by the Kitsu themselves. Terraced hills, buckwheat fields, trickling streams, and vast plains roll across Kitsu provinces, dotted by humble shrines, small farming communities, and ancient crumbling tombs. Outsiders traveling through Kitsu territory are immediately regarded with suspicion, as there is little to attract pilgrims or non-Kitsu visitors.

Blue Chrysanthemum Village is the most common destination for travelers permitted to wander Kitsu territory. Named for the unusual blue chrysanthemums that grow throughout it in autumn, the village is famous throughout the Empire, and its flowers are prized by ikebana artists. The blooms grow only in this territory. Folktales attribute the flowers to two star-crossed lovers, a Kitsu and a Doji, whose marriage arrangement was severed by the Lion Clan Champion. Shamed by the couple's protest suicide, the Champion relinquished his titles and became a monk, erecting a shrine to the Fortune Benten in atonement. The monk mingled the lovers' ashes and scattered them in the village; locals believe the first blue chrysanthemums grew from those ashes, a sign that the lovers had forgiven the foolish lord.

The Castle of the Swift Sword (Hayaken no Shiro) stands watch over the Lion's northern border at the foot of the Drowned Merchant River, rising above the Plains of Bloodied Honor on a steep, curved foundation of ancient stone. Within this foundation sits the Tomb of the Five, where the sacred bones of the Kitsu family's founders are said to rest in a secret ancestral shrine. Before the fourth century, only a temple stood here, but threats from bandits necessitated expansion into a military outpost, and eventually an entire keep was built to surround the sacred grounds. Today, the Castle of the Swift Sword serves as the official estate of the Akodo War College while also remaining the ancestral seat of the Kitsu shugenja family and the home of its daimyo. This dual nature as both temple and castle reflects the harmonious relationship of its inhabitants: the Kitsu govern their lands from an Akodo court, and students of the greatest war college in Lion lands study warfare on consecrated temple grounds. Overnight visitors from other clans whisper of feeling ill at ease, as though something unseen watched them within their rooms.

The Kitsu Tombs are where the family lays its dead to rest, separate from the Hall of Ancestors where most Lion heroes are enshrined. Though a Kitsu holding, the tombs and the temple above them rest within Matsu territory. The holding's martial appearance has led visitors to mistake it for a small castle within the Kaeru Forest. The Kitsu daimyo keeps an estate on the grounds, and several shugenja and samurai live in the temple's dormitory. The first floor contains the personal library of the Kitsu daimyo, the second floor is a dojo for training both shugenja and their yojimbo, and the third floor -- closest to the Heavens, and thus to the honored dead -- is reserved for meditation and other rites. The splendid temple sits atop layers of catacombs, many dating from before the Empire's founding. The deepest foundations were once sacred to the kitsu people and are among that civilization's few remaining structures. It is within these deepest tombs, far from prying eyes, that the sodan-senzo train to cross into Yomi and Meido, where the deceased await judgment.
